1
00:00:01,940 --> 00:00:03,000
Hello English learners.

2
00:00:03,360 --> 00:00:04,640
Welcome back to EnglishPod.

3
00:00:04,640 --> 00:00:05,160
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:06,300
And I'm Erica.

5
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:08,960
And today we're going to be taking you to the airport.

6
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:09,920
That's right.

7
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:16,060
We're going to listen as a man checks in at the airport and deals with his luggage.

8
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:17,260
That's right.

9
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:23,200
So we're going to be looking at a lot of great stuff that can help you the next time you are at the airport at the check-in counter.

10
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:28,760
So why don't we take a look at one word that we're going to preview today in Vocabulary Preview.

11
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:30,900
Vocabulary Preview.

12
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:35,650
We're going to hear this guy say, I'd like to check three pieces.

13
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:39,800
He's going to check three pieces of what?

14
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:40,770
Pieces of luggage.

15
00:00:41,660 --> 00:00:45,010
So when we talk about luggage, we can say pieces of luggage.

16
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:46,560
That's right.

17
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:48,490
Luggage is a non-count noun.

18
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:52,180
So you can't say one luggage, two luggages, three luggages.

19
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:52,330
That's wrong.

20
00:00:52,670 --> 00:00:52,870
Right.

21
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:56,000
So we use pieces of luggage.

22
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:57,480
Okay.

23
00:00:57,480 --> 00:00:58,670
So three pieces of luggage.

24
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:00,240
Very good.

25
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,640
Let's listen to our dialogue for the first time.

26
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:08,000
We're going to see what happens with this guy at the airport and with his pieces of luggage.

27
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:17,680
Next please.

28
00:01:18,490 --> 00:01:19,330
Hello, sir.

29
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:20,690
May I see your passport, please?

30
00:01:21,010 --> 00:01:21,250
Yes.

31
00:01:22,050 --> 00:01:22,590
Here you go.

32
00:01:23,010 --> 00:01:24,710
Will you be checking any bags today?

33
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:25,350
Yes.

34
00:01:25,650 --> 00:01:26,610
I'd like to check three pieces.

35
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:28,380
I'm sorry, sir.

36
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:35,480
Airline policy only allows two pieces of checked luggage at 20 kilograms each plus one piece of carry-on luggage.

37
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:38,240
I will have to charge you extra for the additional suitcase.

38
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:39,800
What?

39
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:40,800
Why?

40
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:41,620
I'm taking an intercontinental flight.

41
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:44,320
I'm flying 16,000 kilometers.

42
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:47,520
How am I supposed to take only two 20 kilo bags?

43
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:48,520
That's absurd.

44
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:49,510
I'm sorry, sir.

45
00:01:49,730 --> 00:01:50,510
There's nothing I can do.

46
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:53,790
You cannot board the flight with that large bag either.

47
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:58,400
Carry-on bags must fit in the overhead compartment or under your seat.

48
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:00,130
That bag is clearly too big.

49
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:01,070
Now I see.

50
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:03,470
You charge next to nothing for an international ticket.

51
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:07,880
When it comes to charging for any other small thing, you charge an arm and a leg.

52
00:02:07,880 --> 00:02:10,670
So tell me, miss, how much will I have to pay for all of this?

53
00:02:11,670 --> 00:02:12,490
Let's see.

54
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:13,920
$625 US dollars.

55
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:25,200
That's more than my round-trip ticket!

56
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:27,620
All right, well, that's a whole lot of money, isn't it?

57
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,160
Well, I think it's a very common situation.

58
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:34,680
I'm sure many of our listeners can relate to this situation.

59
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:36,720
Overweight charges are pretty expensive.

60
00:02:37,480 --> 00:02:37,740
Very high.

61
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:39,240
I know from personal experience.

62
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,340
But that's not what we want to talk about now.

63
00:02:41,500 --> 00:02:45,560
We want to talk about some really useful vocabulary in Language Takeaway.

64
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:48,660
Language Takeaway.

65
00:02:49,280 --> 00:02:53,960
All right, on Language Takeaway today, we have five words.

66
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:55,300
So why don't we start with the first one?

67
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:59,280
So the woman asked, will you be checking any bags today?

68
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,340
Will you be checking any bags today?

69
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:03,940
To check bags.

70
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Right.

71
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:06,100
So the verb to check.

72
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:12,250
It means to give your bags to the airline, to register them.

73
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:14,890
Okay, so that's what happens with your luggage.

74
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:18,650
You check them, so basically they become responsible for your bags.

75
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:19,960
Right.

76
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:22,680
It reminds me of checking in to a hotel.

77
00:03:22,730 --> 00:03:23,630
Right, very similar.

78
00:03:23,830 --> 00:03:26,290
When you go to a hotel, you register at the hotel.

79
00:03:26,530 --> 00:03:27,330
You check in.

80
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:30,290
In this case, you just check your luggage.

81
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:31,640
You don't check in your luggage.

82
00:03:31,670 --> 00:03:31,810
Right.

83
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:33,570
Okay, so check luggage.

84
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:39,660
So he wanted to check three pieces of luggage, and he also had carry-on luggage.

85
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:41,840
Carry-on luggage.

86
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:43,200
Carry-on.

87
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:45,190
So carry-on luggage, it's pretty simple.

88
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:47,310
That's right, you carry it on yourself.

89
00:03:47,680 --> 00:03:49,890
Exactly, you carry it on to the plane.

90
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:53,150
Now we can talk about carry-on luggage, right?

91
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,680
Or carry-on bag.

92
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:57,960
Right, or a carry-on suitcase.

93
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:00,640
A very small suitcase that can fit under your seat.

94
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:01,640
Exactly.

95
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:03,560
All right, so carry-on luggage.

96
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:08,520
Now he was traveling on an intercontinental flight.

97
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:09,050
Intercontinental.

98
00:04:09,990 --> 00:04:10,630
Intercontinental.

99
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:12,750
We have two words there, inter.

100
00:04:13,990 --> 00:04:14,190
Between.

101
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:14,730
Between, right?

102
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:16,600
And continental.

103
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:17,850
So this sounds like continent.

104
00:04:18,550 --> 00:04:24,130
Okay, so he's traveling maybe from Asia to North America.

105
00:04:24,550 --> 00:04:25,810
Okay, intercontinental.

106
00:04:26,530 --> 00:04:29,370
He's trying to board this intercontinental flight.

107
00:04:29,930 --> 00:04:32,070
So to board a flight.

108
00:04:32,880 --> 00:04:33,490
Board a plane.

109
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:34,810
Board a train.

110
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:37,600
All right, so basically it means to...

111
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:38,010
To get on.

112
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:39,600
To get on.

113
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:40,600
To get on a vehicle.

114
00:04:40,600 --> 00:04:43,470
Okay, so I can say board the ship, board the car.

115
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:45,360
No.

116
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:46,360
No?

117
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:47,360
No.

118
00:04:47,360 --> 00:04:48,360
But board the train.

119
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:48,490
Board the train.

120
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:50,360
Board the bus.

121
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:51,960
All aboard.

122
00:04:51,960 --> 00:04:55,030
That's right, that's why in movies you see that somebody will yell all aboard.

123
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:56,560
Yeah.

124
00:04:56,560 --> 00:04:57,560
In the train station, right?

125
00:04:57,560 --> 00:04:58,560
Exactly.

126
00:04:58,560 --> 00:04:59,560
All aboard.

127
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:06,440
Okay, so board a vehicle and our last phrase for today, overhead compartment.

128
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:06,990
Overhead compartment.

129
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:08,730
Overhead compartment.

130
00:05:09,630 --> 00:05:12,130
All right, let's break this phrase down.

131
00:05:12,910 --> 00:05:13,510
Overhead.

132
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:16,370
So that's on top of your head, right?

133
00:05:16,550 --> 00:05:16,750
Exactly.

134
00:05:17,570 --> 00:05:17,830
Above you.

135
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:23,390
Yeah, and the compartment is a place where you can put something and store it.

136
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:24,640
Kind of like a box.

137
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:25,320
Right.

138
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:27,120
Yeah.

139
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:32,380
So overhead compartment, that big box where you put your bags and your purse or whatever on an airplane.

140
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:33,880
Mm-hmm.

141
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:36,160
Very similar to, for example, an overhead projector.

142
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:41,720
Right, so that's the machine that puts an image or a picture on a wall, like sometimes you see them in meetings.

143
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:46,320
Right, so because the image is over your head and it's big, it's called an overhead projector.

144
00:05:46,710 --> 00:05:48,690
Okay, so some great language.

145
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:54,520
Why don't we hear it again in context by listening to the dialogue for the second time?

146
00:05:59,910 --> 00:06:00,930
Next, please.

147
00:06:01,770 --> 00:06:02,770
Hello, sir.

148
00:06:03,450 --> 00:06:05,310
May I see your passport, please?

149
00:06:05,810 --> 00:06:07,170
Yes, here you go.

150
00:06:07,780 --> 00:06:10,360
Will you be checking any bags today?

151
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:13,820
Yes, I'd like to check three pieces.

152
00:06:14,610 --> 00:06:15,910
I'm sorry, sir.

153
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:24,850
Airline policy allows only two pieces of checked luggage at 20 kilograms each, plus one piece of carry-on luggage.

154
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:29,060
I will have to charge you extra for the additional suitcase.

155
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:30,140
What?

156
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:31,120
Why?

157
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:34,320
I'm taking an intercontinental flight.

158
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:38,280
I'm flying 16,000 kilometers.

159
00:06:38,280 --> 00:06:41,480
How am I supposed to take only two 20-kilo bags?

160
00:06:42,160 --> 00:06:42,280
That's absurd.

161
00:06:43,160 --> 00:06:44,300
I'm sorry, sir.

162
00:06:44,760 --> 00:06:46,240
There's nothing I can do.

163
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:49,700
You cannot board the flight with that large bag either.

164
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:56,080
Carry-on bags must fit in the overhead compartment or under your seat.

165
00:06:56,080 --> 00:06:57,890
That bag is clearly too big.

166
00:06:58,680 --> 00:06:59,210
Now I see.

167
00:07:00,200 --> 00:07:03,830
You charge next to nothing for an international ticket.

168
00:07:04,860 --> 00:07:10,150
But when it comes to charging for any other small thing, you charge an arm and a leg.

169
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:14,690
So tell me, miss, how much will I have to pay for all this?

170
00:07:15,710 --> 00:07:16,510
Let's see.

171
00:07:17,200 --> 00:07:18,200
$625 US dollars.

172
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:19,200
No.

173
00:07:21,240 --> 00:07:23,200
That's more than my round-trip ticket.

174
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:25,360
All right.

175
00:07:28,340 --> 00:07:30,400
So I think it's a little bit more clear now.

176
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:35,960
And with this, it's time for us to take a look at some great phrases in putting it together.

177
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:38,940
Putting it together.

178
00:07:40,270 --> 00:07:44,750
So this guy was pretty angry about this airline policy.

179
00:07:44,750 --> 00:07:48,070
And he said, how am I supposed to?

180
00:07:48,790 --> 00:07:50,590
How am I supposed to?

181
00:07:50,870 --> 00:07:51,950
How am I supposed to?

182
00:07:52,330 --> 00:07:52,570
Okay.

183
00:07:52,730 --> 00:07:59,590
So before we get into explaining this, why don't we listen to a couple of examples so we can try and understand what it means.

184
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:01,940
Example one.

185
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:06,460
How am I supposed to fit the dog in the car?

186
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:11,080
There are 10 people in there already.

187
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:11,340
Example two.

188
00:08:14,320 --> 00:08:16,190
How is she supposed to get to work if her car's in the shop?

189
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:21,040
Example three.

190
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,100
How are we supposed to know the answer to that?

191
00:08:25,480 --> 00:08:26,000
It's impossible.

192
00:08:28,180 --> 00:08:28,620
Okay.

193
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:32,560
So I can understand that it means, how can I do this?

194
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:34,120
Right.

195
00:08:34,120 --> 00:08:35,100
How do you expect me to do this?

196
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:37,040
How is it possible for me to do this?

197
00:08:37,640 --> 00:08:37,840
Very good.

198
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:40,280
It's a phrase you use when you're frustrated, right?

199
00:08:40,680 --> 00:08:41,080
Yeah, exactly.

200
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:47,200
When you're maybe a little bit frustrated or you're maybe nervous of something that you can't do or don't know how.

201
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:48,200
Yeah.

202
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:53,820
This phrase, it's not impolite, but it sort of gives the idea that you're challenging the other person a little bit.

203
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:55,360
Maybe.

204
00:08:55,360 --> 00:08:56,360
Yeah.

205
00:08:56,360 --> 00:08:57,360
Okay.

206
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:02,240
So a good phrase that you can use in different situations and you can try and mix it up with different verbs, right?

207
00:09:02,240 --> 00:09:03,240
Right.

208
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:04,240
All right.

209
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:04,850
Now let's take a look at our next phrase.

210
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:06,800
I'm sorry, sir.

211
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:08,620
There's nothing I can do.

212
00:09:09,240 --> 00:09:10,380
There's nothing I can do.

213
00:09:11,160 --> 00:09:12,420
There's nothing I can do.

214
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:13,380
There's nothing I can do.

215
00:09:13,800 --> 00:09:20,500
So basically, the woman can't help the man, even though he's insisting and he's pushing that he wants another solution.

216
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:21,760
Yeah.

217
00:09:21,760 --> 00:09:22,760
Right.

218
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:26,340
So finally, she says, I'm sorry, there's nothing I can do.

219
00:09:26,340 --> 00:09:30,540
So we use this phrase when someone's pushing you too much, right?

220
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:31,020
Right.

221
00:09:31,140 --> 00:09:35,460
And you would use it only in that situation towards the end because it is a little bit...

222
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:36,140
It's strong.

223
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:41,740
It's not in plight, but it's just, you're saying, you know what, I'm sorry, there's I can't do anything about it.

224
00:09:41,960 --> 00:09:44,000
Now let's move on to our third phrase.

225
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:48,160
You charge next to nothing.

226
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:48,600
Next to nothing.

227
00:09:49,840 --> 00:09:50,620
Next to nothing.

228
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Next to nothing.

229
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:53,000
All right.

230
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:53,470
So we're talking about charging.

231
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:55,920
We're talking about money.

232
00:09:55,920 --> 00:10:02,240
Why don't we listen to some examples of what this next to nothing phrase means?

233
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:02,590
Example one.

234
00:10:06,280 --> 00:10:07,000
Do you like my new house?

235
00:10:07,440 --> 00:10:09,340
It was so cheap that it almost cost next to nothing.

236
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:14,280
Example two.

237
00:10:14,280 --> 00:10:18,880
Let's go to Thailand.

238
00:10:18,880 --> 00:10:20,110
Tickets cost next to nothing right now.

239
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:24,950
Example three.

240
00:10:28,120 --> 00:10:32,080
The real estate agent charged me next to nothing for his commission, so I got a really good deal.

241
00:10:34,030 --> 00:10:37,370
So when something costs next to nothing, it's really cheap, right?

242
00:10:37,370 --> 00:10:39,430
It's really cheap, or you consider it to be cheap.

243
00:10:40,250 --> 00:10:44,010
And now for our last phrase, it's the complete opposite of next to nothing.

244
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:45,800
Right.

245
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:46,830
Charge an arm and a leg.

246
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:48,810
An arm and a leg.

247
00:10:49,080 --> 00:10:50,090
An arm and a leg.

248
00:10:50,510 --> 00:10:51,030
All right.

249
00:10:51,480 --> 00:10:53,230
So this means it's really expensive.

250
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:54,470
It's so expensive.

251
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:56,810
It's like giving your arm and your leg.

252
00:10:57,210 --> 00:10:57,410
Right.

253
00:10:57,530 --> 00:10:58,150
To pay for it.

254
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:00,160
Yeah.

255
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:01,160
Very good.

256
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:02,160
So next to nothing, really cheap.

257
00:11:02,160 --> 00:11:04,190
An arm and a leg, really expensive.

258
00:11:04,920 --> 00:11:05,750
Can we look at the use here?

259
00:11:06,270 --> 00:11:06,850
All right.

260
00:11:06,950 --> 00:11:13,450
So in the dialogue we heard, you charge an arm and a leg, but we can also say it costs an arm and a leg.

261
00:11:13,770 --> 00:11:13,930
Right.

262
00:11:13,990 --> 00:11:15,670
Because we are referring to money.

263
00:11:16,270 --> 00:11:16,390
Right.

264
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:16,970
All right.

265
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:18,150
So some great phrases here.

266
00:11:18,800 --> 00:11:21,570
Now I think it's time we listen to our dialogue one more time.

267
00:11:30,880 --> 00:11:33,120
What?

268
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:35,120
Why?

269
00:11:53,450 --> 00:11:55,010
I'm taking an intercontinental flight.

270
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:57,720
I'm flying 16,000 kilometers.

271
00:11:57,720 --> 00:12:00,920
How am I supposed to take only two 20 kilo bags?

272
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:01,090
That's absurd.

273
